para('24_ch06','The case for staying in code is not sentiment.',r'''For a small team, a documented chain of do-files may be enough to manage the analysis. \textcite{long2009workflow} emphasizes recording the steps from raw files to results so that an analyst can repeat and inspect them. A database can support the same principle when its loading and transformation queries are versioned. Compare the alternatives using the work your team actually performs: the size of joins, how often files arrive, who needs simultaneous access, and who will maintain the system. Include staff time in the cost estimate and retain a reproducible record of cleaning decisions whichever storage system you choose.''','Remove categorical database claims and unsupported salary fraction.')
para('24_ch06','To hold that position credibly',r'''A database becomes worth investigating when routine files exceed available memory, several users need controlled access at the same time, or a data-use agreement requires detailed access logs. Frequent data arrivals may instead be manageable with a scheduled do-file. Diagnose the constraint before choosing the system: a slow manual refresh and a need for row-level access controls are different problems.''','Remove false hard limits and overenumeration.')
para('24_ch06','Table~\\ref{tab:ch06-dbdecision} turns',r'''Use Table~\ref{tab:ch06-dbdecision} to discuss requirements with the team responsible for maintaining the data. A dated extract gives an analysis a stable input even when the database changes, and it can spare collaborators a separate connection setup. If you query a database directly, record the query and preserve the result used for the analysis so that another analyst can reproduce the reported numbers.''','Replace arbitrary prohibitions with reproducible alternative.')
para('24_ch06','\\caption[The database decision in seven questions]',r'''\caption[Questions to ask before adopting a database]{Questions for the evaluation team and the people who would maintain a database. Use the answers to identify the actual constraint, compare alternatives, and budget implementation and continuing support.}''','Remove hard decision algorithm in caption.')
replace('24_ch06','Limits code cannot get around: any yes points to a database','Requirements to investigate before choosing storage','Correct categorical table.')
replace('24_ch06',"Does your largest working file crowd half the machine's memory? & Yes: a real wall",'Do routine joins exceed available memory? & Compare larger-memory and database options','Remove arbitrary half memory cutoff.')
replace('24_ch06','Do data arrive faster than a person can rerun the build? & Yes: a real wall','Do data arrive faster than the manual refresh? & Consider a scheduled build','Correct schedulable process.')
replace('24_ch06','Does an agreement require a log of who read which rows? & Yes: a real wall','Does an agreement require row-level access logs? & Consult the data steward on supported systems','Make access requirement actionable.')
replace('24_ch06','Conditions that must all hold before buying helps','Implementation and maintenance questions','Remove categorical claim.')
replace('24_ch06','Is a quarter to half of a salary funded, permanently, to run it? & No: fix this first','Is continuing technical support funded? & Estimate staff time and assign responsibility','Remove unsupported numeric estimate.')
replace('24_ch06','Will researchers who know the variables still do the cleaning? & No: do not buy','Will domain experts review the cleaning rules? & Document and review transformations','Concrete review.')
replace('24_ch06','Can a definition change land in days, not weeks? & No: do not buy','Can staff revise a definition and reproduce old results? & Version queries and retain dated extracts','Remove arbitrary deadline.')
replace('24_ch06','Can every tool read the output without a fragile driver? & No: write files instead','Can collaborators read the output with their tools? & Test connections or supply dated files','Remove prescription.')
replace('31_ch07','Alpha is not a fixed target: it rises mechanically with the number of items, so a long scale can clear 0.80 on padding alone. The conventional floor is 0.70 for research and 0.80 for high-stakes decisions, but a value above 0.95 usually signals redundant items, not virtue.','Alpha depends on both the number of items and their covariance. Interpret it alongside the content of the questions and the intended use of the score; a high value can reflect repetitive items and cannot by itself establish validity.','Remove arbitrary alpha permissions.')
